For a WordPress.org submission, disclose every third-party service in `readme.txt`, run Plugin Check before upload, place display images in `assets/`, and declare a GPL-compatible license consistently in both the plugin header and readme.[1][2][3][4]

## External service disclosure

- Clearly explain that the plugin relies on a third-party service, when it does, which servers it calls, and whether an account or API key is required.[4]
- Include links to the service and its Terms of Use and/or privacy policy.[1][4]
- Make external calls as securely as possible; remote code loading is allowed only for documented services.[1]
- If the plugin is fundamentally a service interface, the readme must document the service in enough detail that users understand what they are installing before installation.[1][4]

**Official URL:** [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/detailed-plugin-guidelines/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/detailed-plugin-guidelines/)[1]

## Plugin Check

Plugin Check tests whether a plugin meets WordPress.org directory standards and flags issues involving security, accessibility, performance, internationalization, and development best practices.[2]

**Official URL:** [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/developer-tools/helper-plugins/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/developer-tools/helper-plugins/)[2]

## Assets

- Put plugin headers, icons, and screenshots in the root-level `assets/` directory of the SVN checkout; these assets apply across plugin versions.[3]
- Provide one screenshot for each screenshot line in `readme.txt`; screenshot captions come from those lines.[3]
- Screenshots must be local, not loaded from an external domain.[3][4]

**Official URL:** [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/plugin-assets/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/plugin-assets/)[3]

## License compatibility

- Plugins must be compatible with GPLv2 or later; “GPLv2 or later” is recommended.[1][5]
- All code, data, and images stored in the WordPress.org-hosted plugin directory must be GPL or GPL-compatible, including bundled third-party libraries and assets.[1][4]
- Declare the same license in both the plugin header and `readme.txt`; document any third-party components and their licenses.[4]

**Official URLs:**  
- [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/detailed-plugin-guidelines/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/detailed-plugin-guidelines/)[1]
- [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/common-issues/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/common-issues/)[4]

## Readme requirements

Use a standards-compliant `readme.txt`—not `readme.md`—and ensure it validates with the WordPress readme Validator.[4][6]

Include:

- A concise description of what the plugin does.[7]
- Installation and configuration instructions, including a link to service registration if required.[7]
- Support information and scope of support.[7]
- Full service disclosure for external calls: service purpose, servers contacted, account requirements, and terms/privacy links.[4]
- A declared GPL-compatible license.[4][6]

**Official URLs:**  
- [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/how-your-readme-txt-works/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/how-your-readme-txt-works/)[6]
- [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/planning-submitting-and-maintaining-plugins/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/planning-submitting-and-maintaining-plugins/)[7]
- [https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/common-issues/](https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/common-issues/)[4]

